  #3. SemanticIssueTracker (step 3 of 4) is implemented and wired in.

  New file src/utils/semantic_issue_tracker.py — deterministic, mirrors the IssuePatternTracker style. It is the semantic analogue of steps 1-2: where those track syntax errors, this
  tracks UNSATISFIABLE PREDICATES and ASSERTION COUNTEREXAMPLES across iterations — the issues that, when persistent, indicate a requirement-level problem (the 0618 run's
  EmergencyBypassFIFOScenario stayed UNSAT for 9+ iterations with model-only fixes and was never escalated).

  Key concept — "measurable" iterations: an iteration whose model failed to parse (syntax != OK) tells us nothing about whether a semantic issue persists; it neither extends nor breaks a
  persistence run. Only syntax-OK iterations count. This matters because real runs interleave syntax-error iterations with semantic-issue iterations (0618: syntax error at 17, UNSAT at
  18-21 — consecutive count is still 4).

  Inputs: current iteration, current VerificationResult, all regression-log entries (their current_result.unsatisfied_predicates / .counterexamples lists, which the executor already
  populates). Output stored on the entry as semantic_issue_persistence:
    { "issues": [{name, kind, consecutive, total, iterations, first_seen}], "escalated_issues": [...], "escalation_required": bool }
  Thresholds (constructor args): consecutive >= 3 measurable iterations OR total >= 4 in a 10-iteration window -> escalation_required.

  Wiring: instantiated in workflow.py __init__, called in _step4 right after IssuePatternTracker; stored on RegressionLogEntry (new field, to_dict/from_dict back-compat).

  Note: the originally planned "ResolutionStatusTracker" role (did the previous target issue get resolved?) was already covered by the existing check_issue_resolved /
  resolved_target_issue mechanism; step 3 was re-scoped to semantic persistence, which was the actual gap.

  #4. RepairPlateauDetector (step 4 of 4) is implemented and wired in — and the chain is now CONSUMED, not write-only.

  New file src/utils/repair_plateau_detector.py — deterministic. Converts the chain's classifications into escalation decisions plus data-only "directive" evidence blocks that are
  injected into the agents' prompts. Facts live in Python; the behavioral rules live in the prompt files (project convention: no hardcoded prompts in Python).

  build_syntax_escalation(iteration, issue_pattern, error_signature, entries) maps pattern_type to a strategy:
    same_error_persisted      -> level 1 FORBID_PRIOR_FIXES
    recurring_same_error      -> level 2 BREAK_OSCILLATION
    alternating_error_loop    -> level 2 BREAK_OSCILLATION (directive names the oscillation-partner signature from the N-1 entry)
    repair_plateau            -> level 3 REWRITE_BLOCK (rewrite_block=True)
  The directive lists: pattern, error signature, affected block, iterations where the same error occurred, and every fix already attempted — both the Evaluator's [FIX INTENT] lines
  (parsed from stored evaluator_feedback of matched iterations) and the RE agent's applied fix_intent (from the entry at matched_iteration+1, since iteration i's fix is applied when
  creating iteration i+1's model).

  build_semantic_escalation(iteration, semantic_persistence, entries) -> level 3 REQUIREMENTS_DIAGNOSIS when escalation_required, with a per-issue evidence block (occurrence counts,
  iterations, attempted fixes).

  Consumption (this is what makes steps 1-4 change behavior):
  - Evaluator, syntax path: workflow _step5_6 builds the escalation, stores it on entry.repair_escalation, and passes the directive as pattern_status to
    GenerateSyntaxRepairInstruction (all three call sites, incl. the user-guidance variant). Both syntax-repair prompt sections gained a "CROSS-ITERATION ERROR PATTERN STATUS" block
    with binding STRATEGY RULES (FORBID_PRIOR_FIXES: all listed fixes forbidden; BREAK_OSCILLATION: one change resolving both errors; REWRITE_BLOCK: no more local patches — instruct a
    from-scratch rewrite of the affected block). The failed-attempt loading in the retry loop now also unions the IssuePatternTracker's matched iterations (location-independent) with
    the symbol-based index.
  - Evaluator, semantic path: when escalated, the new [SECTION: PersistentIssueEscalation] is appended to the GenerateSemanticFeedback prompt (same mechanism as
    QAContextReuseAndUpdate). It makes "escalate to requirement clarification" mandatory for the escalated issues and requires a full diagnosis in the EXISTING feedback sections
    (=== POTENTIAL REQUIREMENT ISSUES ===: classification inconsistency/ambiguity/gap + evidence + minimal conflicting requirement set / ambiguous text + interpretations;
    === REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===: the concrete replacement wording, which _step7 already feeds into the English requirements). Model-overconstraint is the justified exception routed to
    REPAIR INSTRUCTIONS. Forbidden: repeating attempted fixes, converting failing assertions to facts, "keep fix" without diagnosis. RefineFeedback is instructed to preserve the
    diagnosis through user review; the anti-assert-masking rule was also added to the always-on DECISION POLICY.
  - RE agent: _step8 reads the previous iteration's entry.repair_escalation and passes the directive to UpdateAlloyModel as escalation_directive; UpdateAlloyModel_Base renders it, and
    Mode1 gained an ESCALATION OVERRIDE (REWRITE_BLOCK lifts the minimal-change constraint for the named block only; listed fixes may not be re-applied) while Mode2's Forbidden Changes
    gained the same re-apply/assert-masking prohibitions.

  Verified by test/test_escalation_chain.py (0618 replay -> escalation fires at 3rd persistent iteration; plateau -> REWRITE_BLOCK; A-B-A-B -> BREAK_OSCILLATION; no premature
  escalation; entry round-trip incl. old logs; all modified prompt templates render). Existing regression-log test suites still pass.

  #5. Semantic diagnostics — rungs 2-3 of the semantic escalation ladder (scope/trace sweep + fact localization).

  New file src/utils/semantic_diagnostics.py — deterministic and LLM-free. When a predicate stays UNSAT past the SemanticIssueTracker thresholds, the Analyzer now answers empirically
  BEFORE the Evaluator is forced into requirements diagnosis:

  - Rung 2, scope sweep: the stuck predicate's run command is isolated (all other run/check commands commented out with a //DIAG marker) and re-run with every bound in its scope clause
    doubled (capped at 16; " for 8" appended if unbounded). SAT at the larger scope proves the "persistent UNSAT" was a bounded-search artifact — the correct action is scope
    adjustment, not a requirements escalation — and rung 3 is skipped.
  - Rung 3, fact localization (greedy delta debugging): starting from all facts disabled (if STILL UNSAT -> verdict internal_contradiction: the conflict is inside the predicate body or
    signature declarations), each fact is tentatively disabled; facts whose absence leaves the predicate UNSAT are exonerated. What remains is a 1-minimal blocking fact set. Because
    constructs are R-prefixed (fact R1R2_...), the set maps directly to requirement IDs — the directive carries a PROVEN minimal conflict set instead of LLM guesses. Budget:
    max 12 runs/predicate, max 2 predicates (excess reported as "diagnosis capped"); budget exhaustion or failed variants mark the result approximate (facts kept conservatively).

  Every Alloy run goes through an injected check(model_text, predicate) -> True(SAT)/False(UNSAT)/None(variant failed) callable, so the logic is unit-testable without Alloy;
  make_alloy_sat_checker is the production adapter (writes diag_N.als variants, runs AlloyExecutor, reads unsat_run_commands/has_results).

  Wiring: Workflow._run_semantic_diagnostics, called inside the level-3 semantic escalation branch of _step5_6 (before the entry is saved). It filters escalated issues to kind
  unsat_predicate (counterexamples are not diagnosable by these rungs), appends the rendered "DETERMINISTIC DIAGNOSIS" evidence block to the escalation directive, and stores raw
  results as repair_escalation['diagnostics']. Best-effort: any failure (e.g. missing Alloy jar) logs and leaves the escalation unchanged.

  Prompt rules (facts in Python, behavior in prompts): the PersistentIssueEscalation section now instructs — SAT-at-enlarged-bounds exempts that issue from the mandatory diagnosis
  (decision MUST be adjust scope/trace and rerun with the swept bounds); a proven blocking set must ground the diagnosis in exactly those requirement IDs (measured beats hypothesized);
  internal_contradiction routes to the model-overconstraint exception unless the predicate transcribes requirement text.

  Bug fixed while testing: enlarge_run_scope doubled EVERY digit on the run line, mangling digit-bearing predicate names (run R1R2_Conflict for 4 -> run R2R4_Conflict for 8); it now
  rewrites only the scope clause after `for`.

  Verified by test/test_semantic_diagnostics.py (15 tests: extraction/disable/isolate helpers, scope enlargement incl. the name-mangling regression, minimal-pair localization in 4
  runs, internal contradiction, budget exhaustion -> approximate, failed variants kept conservatively, artifact short-circuits rung 3, directive rendering, adapter SAT/UNSAT/None
  mapping). test_escalation_chain.py still passes (prompt renders).

  #6. Requirement-change decision gate + targeted regeneration — rung 5 of the semantic escalation ladder (ladder complete).

  The gate rides the EXISTING _step5_6 user-review interaction rather than adding a new prompt: when a level-3 semantic escalation fired and the draft feedback contains
  === REQUIREMENT UPDATES ===, a banner prints the proposed update(s) and the single review input doubles as the decision. classify_gate_decision (repair_plateau_detector.py,
  deterministic) maps it: bare accept/yes/ok -> accepted; bare reject/no -> rejected (bare keywords are consumed by the gate, NOT sent to RefineFeedback); any substantive text ->
  edited (flows through RefineFeedback as before, treated as approval with modifications); empty/timeout -> provisional.

  Enforcement — Workflow._apply_requirement_gate, called in BOTH the refine and draft paths before the final feedback is stored:
  - rejected: remove_requirement_updates deterministically strips the === REQUIREMENT UPDATES === section (header to next === header or EOF; singular/plural variants) so
    _step7_update_requirements cannot apply it regardless of LLM behavior; the rejection is stored as a confirmed QARecord for future retrieval. Escalation stays at level 3.
  - accepted / edited / provisional: the decision is stored as a QARecord (timeout -> status provisional, source agent_assumption, per the agreed design), and
    build_regeneration_escalation overwrites entry.repair_escalation with a level-5 REGENERATE_PREDICATES escalation. Regeneration targets = the escalated predicates PLUS the proven
    blocking facts from the rung-3 diagnosis when one exists; the directive lists the user decision, the targets, the implicated requirement IDs, and the approved update text
    (facts only - rules live in the prompts).

  Consumption: _step8 carries the previous iteration's escalation as before; REGENERATE_PREDICATES was added to the strategies that get the LATEST REQUIREMENTS attached, and
  RE_prompt.txt Mode2 gained an ESCALATION OVERRIDE rule - DELETE each listed construct and rebuild it from the UPDATED requirements document (not from the old text, which resisted
  repair), preserving names/parameters unless feedback says otherwise, focused-change constraint lifted for the listed constructs and their call sites only, regenerated constructs
  must encode exactly the implicated requirements as now worded.

  Verified by test/test_requirement_gate.py (5 tests: keyword/timeout/edited classification incl. "no, but change R3 too" -> edited; section stripping incl. last-section and
  singular-header variants and absent-section no-op; regeneration escalation with/without rung-3 diagnostics; edited/provisional directive wording). Escalation-chain and
  semantic-diagnostics suites still pass.

  Known cosmetic quirk: a bare "accept" skips RefineFeedback and uses the draft as final, so the console prints "No user feedback provided" after the decision line.

  Remaining on this ladder: live-run validation only — rungs 2-3 and 5 have never been exercised against a real Alloy/LLM run.
